It is common for freshwater lakes near or above a latitude of 45° north of the equator, like Lake Mjgsa in Norway, to
accumulate surface ice in winter. The amount and duration of ice depends on many factors, including local weather
conditions as well as the lake''s depth, volume, and surface area, but a climate researcher claims that some lakes in these
latitudes have seen a decline in the duration of ice between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s. She cites as a typical
example', '["both Lake Neusiedl and Oulujarvi, which had fewer than 195 days of ice in the winter of 1980-81.", "Lake Neusiedl, which had more days of ice in the winter of 2005-06 than it did in the winter of 1980-81.", "Oulujarvi, which had fewer days of ice in the winter of 2005-06 than it did in the winter of 1980-81.", "both Lake Neusiedl and Oulujarvi, which had more than 105 days of ice in the winter of 2005-06."]', 2, 'The correct answer is C. This question requires understanding of command of evidence concepts.', 'This question tests your understanding of Command of Evidence. Information and Ideas is one of the key content domains on the SAT.', 'Practice more Command of Evidence problems to strengthen your skills in this area.', 0, 120, '[]');
